The LitDict.global file format
==============================
Logon uses a proprietary LitDict.global file (the format origins from the Vision project) which 
contains the translations in the following way: the messages in all languages are kept within a single file, 
each translation below each other. e.g.:

	#####################################################
	# Some comment
	#####################################################

	application.entry.welcome:
	[de]Willkommen zu LOGON (Logistics Online)
	[en]Welcome to LOGON (Logistics Online)
	[fr]Bienvenue dans LOGON (logistique on-line)
	[it]Benvenuti su LOGON (Logistics Online)

	application.help:
	[de]Hilfe
	[en]Help
	[fr]Aide
	[it][TODO]Help
	
The optional [TODO] tag means that the entry is not yet translated or verified. (The generated LitDict.properties 
files will not contain this [TODO] tag) If needed, the tag can contain additional freetext info.(e.g. author, 
ticket number, or anything) e.g.: [fr][TODO translate/2009.11.12]French 	
	

LitDict related Ant targets in logon-common
===========================================
A) adnant generatelitdict

This target is automatically called during the build of the project, and calls the LitDictExport.groovy script 
which generates the standard LitDict.properties files for each language into the logon-common/build/generated/litdict directory.
These property files are then copied by Ant into the logon-common.jar. The script also generates a CSV file 
which could be periodically sent to Crh when translations are needed.

B) adnant importlitdict

This target calls the LitDictImport.groovy script. It can parse the response CSV files (corrections 
arriving from the Crh) and generate a file which have the LitDict.global format.
(Note: in fact, the CSV is a tab delimited txt file in our case)

Detailed usage of the import:

1. Copy the file to be imported in format 'TAB-delimited text file' over LitDict.replace_with_latest_to_be_imported.txt
   (Alternative: Adjust build.xml of logon-common)
   - Saving in Excel has a problem: It adds " characters around values which contain a "!
     This is nonsense, because we use TAB-delimited format exactly to avoid this!
     Alternative: use a macro, as appended below.
   - Use Open Office, which allows you to configure the export format: "Tab"-seperated, and no quoting for text values.
2. Execute the import command with 'adnant importlitdict'
   Troubleshooting / common problems in the file to be imported:
   - The file must be TABULATOR seperated - chose the right type in Excel
   - Comment lines must start with a #
   - "Empty lines" are forbidden! (Excel sometimes writes a last line, containing only tabs) => Remove that!
   - ...
3. The imported LitDict.global file will appear in the build/generated/litdict directory, the filename will 
   have the "LitDictImported_YYYY-MM-DD_HH-mm.global" pattern. 
4. Take a deep and thorough look on this generated file (e.g. diff with the head version of LitDict.global) and if everything 
   seems to be OK, the head version of LitDict.global can be overwritten with the generated file 
   (or, instead of overwriting, the changes can be manually copied with the diffing tool too).

   Things to pay attention to:
   - Since cluster 2 LOGON uses the UTF-8 format! Check for special characters (e.g. cyrillic letters)
   - Especially dangerous: Word/Excel turn "..." into the special single character with 3 dots! => Manually fix them in the LitDict.global!
   - (add more findings to this README)

----

Macro to save a file in TAB-delimited text format in Excel without "-quoting
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Public Sub TextNoModification()
        Const DELIMITER As String = "," 'or "|", vbTab, etc.
        Dim myRecord As Range
        Dim myField As Range
        Dim nFileNum As Long
        Dim sOut As String
        
        nFileNum = FreeFile
        Open "C:\Users\Roland Schuler\Desktop\Test.txt" For Output As #nFileNum
        For Each myRecord In Range("A1:A" & _
                Range("A" & Rows.Count).End(xlUp).Row)
            With myRecord
                For Each myField In Range(.Cells(1), _
                        Cells(.Row, Columns.Count).End(xlToLeft))
                    sOut = sOut & DELIMITER & myField.Text
                Next myField
                Print #nFileNum, Mid(sOut, 2)
                sOut = Empty
            End With
        Next myRecord
        Close #nFileNum
    End Sub
